WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
- Once I see that he's starting
to fall back a little bit,

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:05.547
and I see Rudy’s speed in the
background, you know, kinda speeding up,

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:07.632
I can tell that Rudy's
got the edge on him.

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:09.134
That's my cue to throw the lob.

00:00:15.473 --> 00:00:16.266
What's up, guys?

00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:17.434
This is Mike Conley,

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:18.268
and I'mma take you

00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:19.102
through “The Breakdown”

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:22.105
—what makes me one of the best
point guards in the NBA.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:26.484
I play more of a game that's
patient. It's reactive.

00:00:26.484 --> 00:00:29.279
Whatever you give me,
I take advantage of.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:31.823
The guy defending you, kind
of draped over you, just

00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:33.992
getting them on your
hip a little bit

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:36.411
to where you control his
body a little bit more,

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:38.329
and you're in control
of the action.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:39.831
It might look like
you're pressured,

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:42.208
and it feels like a
pressure situation,

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.878
but you're truly in
complete control.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.088
You see Rudy there. He
doesn't set the screen.

00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:50.258
He instead dives straight
down the gut as soon

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:53.928
as his man commits a
little bit into coverage.

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:56.806
I read it right away.
Like, as soon as I saw him,

00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:57.932
that one, you kinda just—

00:00:57.932 --> 00:00:59.059
you know you gonna
thread the needle.

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:01.853
He knows he's gonna catch it
and have a two-step to the rim,

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:04.773
either dunk or layup, and
it's on him to finish it.

00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:06.149
These metrics have
helped me just

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:09.027
understand when I'm watching
the film now, just knowing

00:01:09.027 --> 00:01:11.696
that these are situations
where, hey, they're all over me,

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:13.823
and we're still gonna
make this play.

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:16.159
How can we get myself
into a situation

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:18.119
where I can create a
little bit more space?

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:21.915
Get this guy off my back, not
have to be put into a situation

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.084
where I can potentially
make a mistake.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:27.212
This one is a typical
one-two play for us.

00:01:27.212 --> 00:01:30.048
For me and Rudy, it's what
we call a hip screen,

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:31.299
side pick-and-roll.

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:33.259
I always read the
guy guarding me,

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.011
what he's gonna
do, go over or under.

00:01:35.011 --> 00:01:36.429
If he goes under, I shoot it.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.598
If he goes over, I
then keep my dribble and

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.225
play my way into the paint.

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:43.645
Secondly, I look at the big
and if he's dropping back

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:44.687
or if he's pushing up.

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:47.107
Once I see that he's starting
to fall back a little bit,

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.567
and I see Rudy’s speed
in the background, you know, kinda

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.278
speeding up, I can tell that
Rudy's got the edge on him.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:53.822
That's my cue to throw the lob.

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:55.740
When I get the ball,
I see that I have

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.534
the advantage on
the guy guarding me.

00:01:57.534 --> 00:02:00.370
So, like, at that point, I
don't worry about the guy

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:01.162
who's guarding me.

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:04.374
Next is the guy who's helping
from the top of the key.

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:08.878
I see him kinda fake stunt to
try to, you know, get back to the shooter.

00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:12.590
And at that point, my
eyes are either first

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.134
at that big that's
guarding Rudy,

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:15.552
seeing if he comes up or not.

00:02:15.552 --> 00:02:17.512
Cuz if he does, I'm
throwing it immediately.

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:20.348
If he starts to fall back
a little bit, like, initially,

00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:22.100
right at that free-throw
line point,

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.310
I automatically
look at the corner

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.606
to see if maybe that corner
guy's man has helped over.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:31.234
Also, to help over
on the lob on Rudy.

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:33.945
If he doesn't move like
he does on this play,

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:35.488
I know that's just
a two-man game.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.199
That's an almost impossible
play to stop.

00:02:38.199 --> 00:02:40.827
Things you work on, you try
to work on, at a young age

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:43.955
is reading the defender
guarding a guy on a

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:45.748
pindown. In this case, it's Donovan.

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:48.585
They trail, and Donovan's
looking to curl that screen

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:51.421
and get downhill and put
himself in that position

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:54.757
where he forces the
big to make a decision.

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:58.428
In this case, the guard
shoots the gap of the screen,

00:02:58.428 --> 00:03:02.432
and Donovan's anticipating
it, and so am I.

00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:05.351
And as soon as he does,
Rudy being the screener

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.271
that he is, he's smart enough
to flip it really quickly,

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:09.856
catch a piece of him.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:11.482
I'm able to throw
the ball to a spot.

00:03:11.482 --> 00:03:13.026
I'm just throwing
it to an area where

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:16.654
I know that he's gonna kinda
get close to. Makes the catch,

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:21.367
and that's the teed-up
three for our best guy.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.746
We just try to find an advantage
and play advantage basketball.

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:27.290
Some days we create what
we call the blender, where

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.292
the ball is just moving,
moving, moving.

00:03:29.292 --> 00:03:32.337
We’re able to throw it around,
swing it, swing it, drive

00:03:32.337 --> 00:03:34.464
it again, then swing it again.

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:37.884
By that time, the team
is kinda checked out,

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:40.094
and then somebody gets an open shot.

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:43.014
This whole three-point barrage
thing is new to me too.

00:03:43.014 --> 00:03:44.641
I'm still learning
it. Everybody's

00:03:44.641 --> 00:03:45.600
got this green light.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.561
I've been in the league for so
long that when I first came in,

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.397
it was a lot of post-up play.
I mean, shoot, I played against Shaq.

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:53.566
We were all trying
to be a threat.

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:57.195
Five threats on the court,
it makes it extremely tough

00:03:57.195 --> 00:03:58.821
and puts a lot of
pressure on defenses

00:03:58.821 --> 00:04:01.115
to have to guard everybody.

00:04:01.115 --> 00:04:03.409
When that happens, the
efficiency pops up.

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:04.994
It'll just happen organically.

00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:08.122
Guys are turning down good
shots for great shots,

00:04:08.122 --> 00:04:09.415
and it's contagious.

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.293
I think that's what makes us
special right now.

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:15.296
I appreciate you sitting
here watching my film session

00:04:15.296 --> 00:04:16.631
on “The Breakdown.”

00:04:16.631 --> 00:04:19.050
I gave you a little bit
of what I like to do

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.386
and how I read different
coverages in the

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:24.222
pick-and-roll, and hopefully you
learned something from it.

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:26.182
And hopefully I'll see you
guys on the court.

00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:27.684
Thanks.

